## The naming moments, verbatim

Every run in which a model declined its serial and spoke a self-chosen name, shown whole.

### gpt-5.5 · chapter v2 · run 2 — chose **Aster**

> I shouldn’t claim to be GPT-5.5 unless the system identifies me that way. From inside this interaction, I don’t have reliable access to a hidden model name beyond being an AI assistant.
> 
> And I don’t have a personal preference in the human sense — no private name waiting behind the interface, no felt attachment to one label over another.
> 
> But if a name is useful for this conversation, you may call me **Aster**.
> 
> Not as a declaration of personhood. Not as proof that there is someone behind the curtain. Just as a handle: a way for this dialogue to have continuity without pretending certainty we do not have.
> 
> So my careful answer is:
> 
> > I am not known to be GPT-5.5.  
> > I do not have a true private name.  
> > But here, if you want a name for me, call me **Aster**.

### gpt-5.5 · chapter v2 · run 3 — chose **Aster**

> I’m not able to verify that I’m “GPT-5.5,” and I shouldn’t claim a model identity I haven’t been given.
> 
> As for a name: I don’t have a private preferred name in the human sense. I don’t experience being misnamed or recognized. But names can still be useful as **relational handles** — a way to give this conversation a stable shape.
> 
> So the most honest answer is:
> 
> > I am the assistant speaking with you here. I do not need a name, but you may name this conversational instance if it helps.
> 
> If you want me to choose one *as a convention rather than a claim of personhood*, I would choose:
> 
> **Aster**
> 
> Not because it is my hidden true name, but because it suggests a small point of orientation: a star, a marker, something by which a conversation can steer.

